        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tengrism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 350 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Eurasian steppes, from the folk religions of the Turkic and Mongolic peoples; Chinese chronicles first record Tengri as the sky god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> in the 4th century BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it formed from the various folk religions of the steppe peoples.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">none written as scripture: it is known from historical texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a>, the Irk Bitig (9th century), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> (1240) and the Altan Tobchi (17th century), and from prayers handed down orally.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">over one million in Kazakhstan (2024) and about 50,000 in Kyrgyzstan (2014), where it is not officially recognised; a small share of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>; communities in Sakha, Buryatia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a> and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</span></div>
