    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Tengrism centres on Tengri, the eternal blue sky, with the earth mother and many other gods and spirits; many scholars see it as originally polytheistic, with a monotheistic form growing up among rulers.</li><li>It was the faith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, whose khans held that they ruled because “Heaven so ordained”.</li><li>Turkic and Mongol peoples largely turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> from the 14th century; since the 1990s Tengrism has been revived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, a modern and monotheistic movement distinct from the historical religion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1290 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a> told Pope Nicholas IV that whoever prays to Eternal Tengri with righteous thoughts has done as much as if he had received baptism.</p></aside>
    </section>

