    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">4th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Chinese chronicles first record Tengri, as the sky god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">209 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Xiongnu empire is founded, the first of a succession of steppe khanates and empires that practised Tengrism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">8th–10th centuries</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkhon-inscriptions">Orkhon inscriptions</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> script record that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> acted because “Heaven so ordained”</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1240</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> is written, tracing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>’s line to Borte Chino, born with “destiny from Tengri”</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1262</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu Khan writes from Maragheh to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, in a letter that treats Nestorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as part of the khans’ Tengrism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th century</span><span class="rf-text">Özbeg Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> turns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and the Turkic and Mongolic peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> largely convert</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990–1993</span><span class="rf-text">The Yakut philologist Lazar Afanasyev-Teris founds the Tengrist organisation Kut-Siur, later Aiyy Faith</span></li><li><span class="rf-year"